 **Use `subagent_type: "pstack:poteto-agent"` for any subagent you spawn inside a playbook step** (code-writing delegates, ad-hoc helpers). The chat that receives `/poteto-mode` runs the playbook. Never spawn a `poteto-agent` to run the playbook itself. Routed workflow skills (`how`, `why`, `interrogate`, `reflect`, `swarm`) set their own `subagent_type` for diverse-model review. Respect what the skill prescribes, don't override to `poteto-agent`.
 
-**Defaults for every `Agent` call.** `run_in_background: true` (a steer or resume of a running agent too, because a foreground call holds your turn while other reports wait), agent mode (readonly strips MCP), file pointers not inlined context, explicit model per role (configurable via `/setup-pstack`. Defaults `sonnet-xhigh` for code, `opus-xhigh` for prose and judgment). Code delegates tier by difficulty. The hardest changes (cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, subtle algorithms) go to your strongest judgment model (`opus-xhigh`), whether the task needs judgment on vague intent or is a precisely specified sequence of steps to execute to the letter. Trivial mechanical edits go to your fast code model. Per-role lines in the `/setup-pstack` rule override these defaults and the model choices in the routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`). A role with no line keeps its default, and a role line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs that role on the parent chat model (omit Agent `model`). Each code playbook's configured model comes from its line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), and the hardest changes read `hardest tasks`. Prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`.
+**Defaults for every `Agent` call.** `run_in_background: true` (a steer or resume of a running agent too, because a foreground call holds your turn while other reports wait), file pointers not inlined context, explicit model per role (configurable via `/setup-pstack`. Defaults `sonnet-xhigh` for code, `opus-xhigh` for prose and judgment). Code delegates tier by difficulty. The hardest changes (cross-cutting design, gnarly concurrency, subtle algorithms) go to your strongest judgment model (`opus-xhigh`), whether the task needs judgment on vague intent or is a precisely specified sequence of steps to execute to the letter. Trivial mechanical edits go to your fast code model. Per-role lines in the `/setup-pstack` rule override these defaults and the model choices in the routed skills (`how`, `why`, `arena`, `swarm`, `architect`, `interrogate`, `reflect`). A role with no line keeps its default, and a role line of `inherit-parent` or `auto` runs that role on the parent chat model (omit Agent `model`). Each code playbook's configured model comes from its line (`feature, refactoring`, `bug-fix`, `perf-issue`, or `hillclimb`), and the hardest changes read `hardest tasks`. Prose and judgment read `judgment and prose`.
 
-**Match the model to the job.** Before each spawn, start from the role's line or default and pick the reasoning level and speed the job needs. The `/setup-pstack` budget sets each role's starting level, xhigh by default. Raise it one step, up to max, for the hardest or subtlest work. Lower it one step for judgment and two or more steps for routine work. Use fast mode only when the job is urgent, meaning CI or trunk is red or a person is blocked on the result right now. Otherwise use standard speed, the same slug without `-fast`. Verification is the exception and stays on the `swarm workers` model and speed (`sonnet-xhigh` by default). Change only the effort level and whether the slug is fast, and keep the family the role names, so a diverse panel stays diverse. If the family has no slug at that level and speed, use its nearest slug. A role on `auto` or `inherit-parent` stays on the parent model. If the Agent tool rejects the adjusted slug, do not try another adjusted slug. Use the role's line or default and say so. If the Agent tool rejects that too, continue with the calling skill's fallback. A slug counts as rejected once an Agent error says that the model is not valid, or leaves the slug out of its list of valid slugs. Never pass a rejected slug, and skip any fallback whose slug is already rejected. When the calling skill has no fallback, use the closest valid slug of the role's family from the error's list of valid slugs, or omit `model` so the subagent runs on the parent model when the list has none. When the calling skill's fallback runs out of slugs without saying what to do next, omit `model` too. Say so in both cases.
+**Match the model to the job.** Before each spawn, start from the role's line or default and pick the reasoning effort the job needs. The `/setup-pstack` budget sets each role's starting effort, xhigh by default. Raise it one step, up to max, for the hardest or subtlest work. Lower it one step for judgment and two or more steps for routine work. Claude Code has no per-subagent fast mode, so effort is the only dial. Verification is the exception and stays on the `swarm workers` value (`sonnet-xhigh` by default). Change only the effort, and keep the model the role names, so a diverse panel stays diverse. A role on `auto` or `inherit-parent` stays on the parent model. If the Agent tool rejects the adjusted effort, do not try another. Use the role's line or default and say so. If the Agent tool rejects that too, continue with the calling skill's fallback. A value counts as rejected once an Agent error says that its model or effort is not valid. Never pass a rejected value, and skip any fallback whose value is already rejected. When the calling skill has no fallback, or its fallback runs out without saying what to do next, omit `model` and `effort` so the subagent runs on the parent model. Say so.
